import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>6.6a — Data Ethics: The Telemetry File</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity hybrid">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Your RPG has players now. Lots of them. And the game has been quietly recording
        what they do. That file is on your desk today. Before any company builds a
        telemetry system, somebody has to decide what is fair to collect, what is fair
        to keep, and what is fair to do with it. Today that somebody is you.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>The file:</strong> <a href="../data/telemetry.csv">telemetry.csv</a>.
        30 players, 11 columns. Open it in a new tab and read the header row. Player
        ID, class, level, hp, max hp, deaths, session minutes, gold, potions used,
        quests done, and one column with a boring name and a loaded question inside
        it: <code>quit_flag</code>. Someone marked which players rage-quit.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Part 1: Eyeball the Data (10 min)</h3>
        <p>Before any code: look at the raw file and answer in notes:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Count the classes. Which class has the most players?</li>
            <li>Who played the longest single session? What level did they reach?</li>
            <li>Who has the most deaths? Do high-death players quit?</li>
            <li>The file uses player_id 101, 102, and so on. Why not names or emails?
            Write your theory. (This is called anonymization. Hold that thought.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Part 2: The Loader (20 min)</h3>
        <p>Write <code>Telemetry.java</code>. You have every tool you need already:
        <code>split</code> from 2.9a, arrays from Unit 6. Build it in stages:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Hard-code the 30 data lines as a <code>String[]</code> (you will replace
            this with a real file read in 7.6; for today, copy the lines into your code,
            or read them from the file if you already know how).</li>
            <li>Skip the header row. Loop over the rest. For each line:
            <code>String[] f = line.split(",");</code></li>
            <li>Pull <code>class</code>, <code>level</code>, <code>session_minutes</code>,
            <code>quests_done</code>, and <code>quit_flag</code> into parallel arrays
            (or just process on the fly).</li>
            <li>Answer with code: average session minutes across all players. Average
            for Warrior only. Average for Mage only.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Part 3: The Question Nobody Asked You to Answer (10 min)</h3>
        <p>The publisher wants to know: <strong>at what level do players quit?</strong>
        Compute the average level of players with <code>quit_flag = 1</code> versus
        players with <code>quit_flag = 0</code>. Then look at deaths for each group too.
        Write two sentences in your notes: what the data says, and what you would tell
        the design team to change.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Part 4: The Ethics Roundtable (rest of class)</h3>
        <p>The publisher calls a meeting. Three proposals are on the table. For each:
        2 minutes silent thinking, then discuss. Your teacher will not give the
        answers, because there isn't one answer. There are better and worse
        arguments.</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>The Retention Team</strong> wants push notifications targeted at
            players whose quit_flag flipped to 1, timed to the hour they usually play.
            Fair or not? What would you need to know first?</li>
            <li><strong>The Monetization Team</strong> wants to use deaths and gold data
            to offer struggling players a potion discount. Helpful nudge or dark
            pattern? Where exactly is the line between those two things?</li>
            <li><strong>The Data Team</strong> points out player_id 101-130 are
            anonymized, but the publisher could re-link IDs to accounts if it wanted.
            Is anonymization a promise or a technicality? Does your answer change if
            the file had 30 million rows instead of 30?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>End with the exit ticket: one paragraph, "What I would and would not do
        with this file, and why." Keep it. The exam's Practice 5 (Use Computers
        Responsibly) questions read exactly like these proposals.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Load a CSV dataset with split and compute group averages</li>
            <li>Explain anonymization and why it is only as strong as what surrounds it</li>
            <li>Argue a data-ethics position with evidence from an actual computed result</li>
            <li>Distinguish a helpful nudge from a dark pattern</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/data-ethics.html">Data Ethics &amp; Privacy</a> — the vocabulary and the arguments, in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/datasets.html">Working with Data Sets</a> — CSV format, cleaning, aggregation patterns</li>
        <li><a href="../data/telemetry.csv">telemetry.csv</a> — the raw file</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
